"Surah al-Mursalat begins with Allah swearing by the dispatched entities. The response to this oath is the verse, \"Indeed, what you are promised is bound to occur.\" The entities sworn by are mentioned by their attributes: Mursalat (those sent forth), Asifat (those sweeping violently), Nashirat (those spreading), Fariqat (those separating), and Mulqiyat (those delivering). There is disagreement regarding what these are: they could be angels, winds, Quranic verses, the sending of prophets, or inspirations that come to human beings. The clearest view is that all of them are spiritual powers—that is, angels—and that they are sent by Allah.\n\nThe letters \"waw\" and \"fa\" prefixed to these attributes indicate the sequence between the actions. While there is a sequence in those that begin with \"fa\", sequence is not a condition for those that begin with \"waw\". For example, \"sweeping violently\" (asifat) comes after being sent forth, yet \"spreading\" (nashirat) may be concurrent with sweeping violently or separate from it. \"Separating\" (fariqat) comes after spreading, and \"delivering the reminder\" (mulqiyat) comes after all of them.\n\nThe word \"urf\" means \"in succession / one after another\" and is a metaphor derived from a horse's mane. It also carries the meaning of \"goodness\" or \"custom\" (urf / a recognized good deed). \"Nashirat\" means to spread or to resurrect the dead (nushur). \"Fariqat\", on the other hand, means to separate, to distinguish between truth and falsehood.\n\n\"Mulqiyat adh-dhikr\" (those that deliver the reminder) may be the angels who bring revelation, or they could be the angels of inspiration who instill admonition in people, or events. In al-Kashshaf, these verses are interpreted by dividing them into two groups: one group of angels brings the commands and sweeps violently through whatever comes before them; the other group of angels spreads the revelation, revives dead souls, separates truth from falsehood, and delivers the reminder to the prophets. This reminder is divided into two categories, being for \"udhr\" (excuse / justification) and \"nudhur\" (warning). Udhr means accepting the excuse of those who embrace the truth, while nudhur means warning those who accept falsehood.\n\nIn al-Kashshaf, it is also stated that oaths are sworn by the winds of punishment and the winds of mercy. The winds of punishment sweep violently, while the winds of mercy bring life to dead earth and distinguish between the grateful and the ungrateful. Those who recognize Allah's blessing and give thanks seek pardon, while the ungrateful are warned. The most comprehensive and sound interpretation is that these attributes refer to forces sent in succession—that is, the angels.\n\nSubsequently, the \"messengers\" (prophets) and their awaited time, the Day of Resurrection, are mentioned. This day is the Day of Decision, when truth will be separated from falsehood. The verse repeated at the end of each section of the surah, \"Woe that Day to the deniers!\", emphasizes the terror of this day. The word \"wayl\" means the descent of calamity, destruction, torment, or a valley/gate in Hell. With each repetition, it emphasizes a different aspect according to the preceding subject: the first addresses the Day of Decision, the second torment, the third Allah's knowledge and power, and the fourth human helplessness and the denial of Allah's blessings.\n\nIn this section, the expression 'wayl' is used to convey the severity of the terrifying state on that day. The word 'mukadhdhibin' (the deniers) is repeated in each verse and is linked each time to a different subject: such as the Day of Decision, torment, Allah's knowledge and power, human helplessness, and the denial of blessings.\n\nIn verses 21–25, the creation of man is addressed: a 'firm lodging' (the womb), a determined term (birth), and a 'receptacle / gathering place' (kifat) are described. The word 'kifat' means 'gathering place' or 'receptacle'; Elmalılı interprets this as the 'livestock enclosure' in Anatolia where stray livestock are penned. Furthermore, Allah's blessings are recalled through the establishment of firm mountains and the provision of sweet water on earth.\n\nIn verses 29–34, the disbelievers are addressed: they are told, 'Proceed to that which you used to deny,' and the expression 'a three-pronged shadow' is explained. This expression points to the Christian belief in the Trinity (father, son, holy spirit); while some commentators state that it is the shadow of the smoke of Hell, according to Ibn Abbas they are commanded to seek shelter in the three prongs of the cross. That shadow neither provides shade nor protects from the flame; the sparks of Hell are as huge as castles and as numerous as yellow camels.\n\nIn verses 35–40, the frequently repeated expression 'woe that Day to the deniers' draws a contrast between those who deserve this punishment and the state of the believers. The righteous (people of taqwa) are rewarded under shades and among springs, with whatever fruits they desire. They are told, 'Eat and drink with satisfaction for what you used to do.' The criminals, on the other hand, are left for a brief time with the words 'Eat and enjoy yourselves a little,' and then they face torment. When they are commanded to bow down (in ruku'), they do not submit.\n\nThe surah concludes with the question, 'Then in what statement after this (the Quran) will they believe?' Elmalılı points out that Surah al-Mursalat ends here, but that the disbelievers will not be let off on the Day of Decision, and that this subject will continue in Surah an-Naba'."